insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('DPDP Act: what to do now, and how we comply','dpdp-act-readiness','The DPDP Rules were notified on 13 November 2025. Most obligations apply from May 2027. Here is a practical checklist.','<p class="standfirst">India''s Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is now in force in phases. If your software holds citizen, customer or employee data, the clock is running.</p>
<h2>The timeline</h2><p>The DPDP Rules, 2025 were notified on 13 November 2025. The Data Protection Board provisions took effect immediately, Consent Manager registration follows after 12 months, and the main obligations on Data Fiduciaries apply from 13 May 2027.</p>
<h2>What to prepare</h2><ul><li><b>Map your data.</b> List what personal data you hold, where, why and for how long.</li><li><b>Fix notices and consent.</b> Notices must be clear, itemised and available in English or any scheduled language. Consent must be specific and as easy to withdraw as to give.</li><li><b>Handle rights requests.</b> Data Principals can ask to access, correct or erase their data and raise grievances.</li><li><b>Plan for breaches.</b> Breaches must be reported to the Board and to affected people. Have a tested response plan.</li><li><b>Set retention rules.</b> Erase data once its purpose is served, and keep processing logs for at least one year.</li><li><b>Protect children''s data.</b> Verifiable parental consent is needed for users under 18.</li></ul>
<p>Penalties can reach ₹250 crore per instance, so this is a board-level issue.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>How VisionID complies</h2><p>Our products include consent capture with purpose tags, a data-principal request workflow, field-level encryption, role-based access, retention schedules with automatic erasure and audit logs kept for at least one year. Our <b>DPDP readiness audit</b> maps your current systems against the Act and gives you a prioritised plan.</p></div>','Compliance','VisionID Team','published','','DPDP Act: what to do now, and how we comply','The DPDP Rules were notified on 13 November 2025. Most obligations apply from May 2027. Here is a practical checklist.','2026-11-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-11-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('Post-quantum cryptography: why to start now','post-quantum-cryptography','Encrypted data captured today could be decrypted by future quantum computers. Here is how to prepare.','<p class="standfirst">Quantum computers powerful enough to break RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography do not exist yet. Data being stolen today could still be read when they do.</p>
<h2>Harvest now, decrypt later</h2><p>Attackers can record encrypted traffic today and store it. Records with a long life, such as land, birth, tax and health data, need protection for decades, so the risk applies today.</p>
<h2>The new standards</h2><p>In August 2024 NIST published its first post-quantum standards: <b>FIPS 203 (ML-KEM)</b> for key exchange, and <b>FIPS 204 (ML-DSA)</b> and <b>FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA)</b> for digital signatures. Browsers and major libraries already support hybrid key exchange that combines classical and post-quantum algorithms.</p>
<h2>What to do</h2><ul><li><b>Inventory your cryptography.</b> Find every place you use RSA, ECC, TLS, VPNs, signatures and certificates.</li><li><b>Rank by data lifetime.</b> Long-lived data moves first.</li><li><b>Build crypto-agility.</b> Make algorithms configurable instead of hard-coded.</li><li><b>Test hybrid TLS.</b> Pilot it on internal services first.</li><li><b>Ask your vendors.</b> Get their PQC roadmaps in writing.</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><h2>How VisionID complies</h2><p>Our platform keeps cryptography in a central, configurable layer, so algorithms can change without rewriting applications. We are testing ML-KEM hybrid key exchange and ML-DSA signatures, and our <b>cryptography audit</b> produces a full inventory and migration roadmap for your systems.</p></div>','Security','VisionID Team','published','','Post-quantum cryptography: why to start now','Encrypted data captured today could be decrypted by future quantum computers. Here is how to prepare.','2026-10-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-10-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('The role of AI in modern software','ai-in-software','AI is now a feature in most business software, not a separate project. Here is where it helps and where to be careful.','<p class="standfirst">Most business software now includes some AI. The useful question is no longer whether to use AI but which parts of your work it should handle.</p>
<h2>Where AI helps</h2><ul><li><b>Data entry.</b> Reading forms, invoices and certificates, then filling fields automatically.</li><li><b>Search.</b> Finding records by asking in plain language, in English or Hindi.</li><li><b>Prediction.</b> Forecasting demand, collections or stock needs.</li><li><b>Anomaly detection.</b> Flagging unusual transactions, logins or traffic.</li><li><b>Assistants.</b> Helping users complete tasks and answering citizen queries.</li></ul>
<h2>Where to be careful</h2><p>AI makes mistakes. For decisions about people''s money, rights or records, a human should review AI suggestions. Personal data used by AI is still covered by the DPDP Act, so purpose and consent rules apply.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>Our approach</h2><p>VisionID adds AI where it saves real time, keeps a person in the loop for important decisions, logs every AI-assisted action and can run models on-premise so data stays within your infrastructure.</p></div>','AI','VisionID Team','published','','The role of AI in modern software','AI is now a feature in most business software, not a separate project. Here is where it helps and where to be careful.','2026-09-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-09-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('AI in network management: from alerts to answers','ai-in-network-management','Networks produce more data than any team can read. AI turns that data into a few useful alerts.','<p class="standfirst">A medium-sized network produces millions of log lines, flow records and SNMP readings every day. No team can read them all, and AI can help sort them.</p>
<h2>What AI does well</h2><ul><li><b>Baselining.</b> It learns what normal traffic looks like for each link and time of day.</li><li><b>Anomaly detection.</b> It flags unusual spikes, new talkers or odd traffic patterns, which are often the first sign of an attack.</li><li><b>Alert correlation.</b> It groups hundreds of related alarms into one incident with a probable root cause.</li><li><b>Capacity forecasting.</b> It predicts when links or devices will run out of headroom.</li><li><b>Natural-language queries.</b> Engineers can ask "which switches dropped packets last night?"</li></ul>
<h2>Keep humans in charge</h2><p>AI should recommend and explain. Configuration changes on production networks should still be approved by an engineer.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>In NetView</h2><p>NetView applies AI anomaly detection to SNMP, flow and syslog data, correlates alerts into incidents and forecasts capacity, while keeping every change behind an approval workflow.</p></div>','Networks','VisionID Team','published','','AI in network management: from alerts to answers','Networks produce more data than any team can read. AI turns that data into a few useful alerts.','2026-08-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-08-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('AI in eGovernance services in India','ai-in-egovernance-india','From property tax to certificates, AI can make citizen services faster and fairer if it is used carefully.','<p class="standfirst">Indian cities and states deliver millions of services every year. AI can reduce waiting times, catch revenue leakage and help citizens in their own language.</p>
<h2>Practical uses</h2><ul><li><b>Property tax.</b> Comparing satellite or drone imagery with records to find unassessed or under-assessed properties.</li><li><b>Document checks.</b> Reading uploaded documents and flagging missing or mismatched information.</li><li><b>Building plan scrutiny.</b> Automated checks of drawings against bye-laws.</li><li><b>Citizen help.</b> Multilingual chat and voice assistants for status queries.</li><li><b>Grievances.</b> Routing complaints to the right department automatically.</li></ul>
<h2>Principles that matter</h2><p>Government AI must be transparent, explainable and fair. Citizens should know when AI is involved and always have a human route for appeals. Data must stay in India and follow the DPDP Act.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>In our eGovernance Suite</h2><p>We use AI to assist officials, not to replace them: suggestions are shown with reasons, decisions stay with authorised staff and every step is logged.</p></div>','eGovernance','VisionID Team','published','','AI in eGovernance services in India','From property tax to certificates, AI can make citizen services faster and fairer if it is used carefully.','2026-07-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-07-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('Zero Trust starts with onboarding','zero-trust-onboarding','Most breaches use valid credentials. A clean joiner-mover-leaver process is the first line of defence.','<p class="standfirst">Zero Trust means never trusting a user or device by default. In practice, it starts with how people join, change roles and leave.</p>
<h2>Common gaps</h2><ul><li>Former employees whose accounts are still active</li><li>People who change roles and keep their old access</li><li>Shared admin passwords</li><li>Firewall rules nobody remembers creating</li></ul>
<h2>What good looks like</h2><ul><li>Access granted automatically from HR records, based on role</li><li>MFA for every user and SSO for every app</li><li>Access removed the same day someone leaves</li><li>Quarterly access reviews by managers</li><li>Firewall rules with owners, expiry dates and regular review</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><h2>With Shield iD</h2><p>Shield iD automates joiner-mover-leaver workflows, enforces SSO and MFA, and finds risky or unused firewall rules across vendors.</p></div>','Security','VisionID Team','published','','Zero Trust starts with onboarding','Most breaches use valid credentials. A clean joiner-mover-leaver process is the first line of defence.','2026-06-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-06-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('Database migration is a business decision','database-migration','Moving off a legacy database can cut licence costs sharply. Success depends on planning, not tools.','<p class="standfirst">Many organisations still run critical systems on old, expensive proprietary databases. Migrating to an open-source database such as PostgreSQL can greatly reduce licence costs and vendor lock-in.</p>
<h2>Why migrations fail</h2><ul><li>Underestimating stored procedures and business logic in the database</li><li>No plan for validating that data moved correctly</li><li>A single "big bang" cut-over with no rollback</li></ul>
<h2>A safer approach</h2><ul><li><b>Assess.</b> Inventory schemas, code, jobs and integrations.</li><li><b>Convert.</b> Convert schemas and procedures with tools, then review by hand.</li><li><b>Validate.</b> Reconcile row counts, totals and checksums.</li><li><b>Run in parallel.</b> Run both systems side by side before switching.</li><li><b>Cut over.</b> Switch in phases with a tested rollback.</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><h2>Our track record</h2><p>We have migrated large government and enterprise databases holding years of financial and citizen records, with full reconciliation reports at every step.</p></div>','Engineering','VisionID Team','published','','Database migration is a business decision','Moving off a legacy database can cut licence costs sharply. Success depends on planning, not tools.','2026-05-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-05-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('Why paper visitor registers no longer work','qr-biometric-visitor-management','QR invites and ID checks make offices safer and front desks faster. Here is what a modern visitor process looks like.','<p class="standfirst">A paper register cannot tell you who is in the building during an emergency, or whether a visitor was ever there.</p>
<h2>The modern flow</h2><ul><li>The host sends a <b>QR invite</b> before the visit.</li><li>The visitor scans in, and their photo and ID are captured.</li><li>The host gets an instant alert.</li><li>A badge prints with an expiry time.</li><li>Check-out is recorded, so you have a live headcount at all times.</li></ul>
<h2>Privacy matters</h2><p>Visitor data is personal data under the DPDP Act. Collect only what you need, show a clear notice and delete records on a fixed schedule.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>With Workplace iD</h2><p>QR invites, ID capture, watchlists and evacuation reports, with DPDP-aligned notices and retention built in.</p></div>','Identity','VisionID Team','published','','Why paper visitor registers no longer work','QR invites and ID checks make offices safer and front desks faster. Here is what a modern visitor process looks like.','2026-04-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-04-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('Supply-chain visibility in real time','supply-chain-visibility','If you can''t see your stock and shipments live, you are planning with old information.','<p class="standfirst">Many businesses still find out about a stock-out or a late delivery after the customer complains. Real-time visibility changes that.</p>
<h2>The building blocks</h2><ul><li><b>Scan everything.</b> Barcode or RFID at every movement, so stock levels are always current.</li><li><b>Track vehicles.</b> GPS gives live location and arrival estimates.</li><li><b>Prove delivery.</b> Electronic proof of delivery with photo, signature and location.</li><li><b>Manage by exception.</b> Alerts for delays, low stock and route deviations.</li></ul>
<h2>The payoff</h2><p>Lower inventory holding, fewer lost shipments, faster billing and fewer disputes.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>With Logistics & Inventory</h2><p>One control-tower view of warehouses, stock and vehicles, with mobile scanning and e-POD.</p></div>','Logistics','VisionID Team','published','','Supply-chain visibility in real time','If you can''t see your stock and shipments live, you are planning with old information.','2026-03-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-03-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts(title,slug,excerpt,body,category,author,status,feature_image,seo_title,seo_description,created_at,updated_at) values ('What a software audit actually checks','what-a-software-audit-checks','An audit should give you a clear list of risks and a plan to fix them, not a 200-page report.','<p class="standfirst">Before you renew a vendor, scale an application or face a compliance review, an independent software audit tells you where you stand.</p>
<h2>What we review</h2><ul><li><b>Security.</b> OWASP Top 10 issues, secrets in code, outdated libraries.</li><li><b>Code quality.</b> Maintainability, test coverage and documentation.</li><li><b>Architecture.</b> Scalability, single points of failure, backup and recovery.</li><li><b>Data protection.</b> Encryption, access control, logging and DPDP gaps.</li><li><b>Licences.</b> Open-source licence obligations.</li></ul>
<h2>What you get</h2><p>A short executive summary, a risk register ranked by severity and a practical remediation plan with effort estimates.</p>
<div class="callout"><h2>Talk to us</h2><p>Our audit team has reviewed systems handling citizen records and public money. Write to sales@visionid.in to plan an audit.</p></div>','Engineering','VisionID Team','published','','What a software audit actually checks','An audit should give you a clear list of risks and a plan to fix them, not a 200-page report.','2026-02-10T06:30:00.000Z','2026-02-10T06:30:00.000Z') on conflict (slug) do nothing;

notify pgrst, 'reload schema';
